FEATURE 1: TRAINING TAB
Purpose: Browse and perform exercises, filtered by muscle focus area.

Data model (hardcoded in lib/data/seed/exercises.dart):

dart
class Exercise {
  final String id;
  final String name;
  final String muscleGroup;     // e.g. Chest, Back, Legs, Arms, Core, Shoulders, Full Body
  final String exerciseType;  // Gym, Cardio, Bodyweight, Stretching
  final String equipment;       // e.g. "Bodyweight", "Dumbbell", "Machine", "Barbell"
  final String gifAssetPath;    // e.g. "assets/gifs/chest_pushup.gif"
  final String? youtubeUrl;     // optional
  final String instructions;    // short text
}
Provide at least 40 sample exercises across all muscle groups as seed data. Use placeholder asset paths following the naming convention assets/gifs/{muscle}_{exercise}.gif — the user will populate real GIFs later.
Why separate muscleGroup and exerciseType
They are orthogonal dimensions — a "Chest" exercise can be "Gym" (bench press) or "Bodyweight" (push-up), and a "Full Body" exercise can be "Cardio" (burpees) or "Gym" (deadlift). Keeping them as two distinct string fields lets you:

Filter independently: "show me Chest + Cardio" or "show me all Cardio".

Combine later: a multi-select filter or a "Gym only" toggle.

Extend without touching the other dimension — add Stretching as a type without re-tagging muscle groups
UI:

Top: horizontal scrollable chip filter row for muscle groups (All, Chest, Back, Legs, ...), plus a secondary filter for equipment (Bodyweight / Dumbbell / Machine).

Below: grid or list of exercise cards showing name, muscle group, equipment, and a static thumbnail (first frame of GIF via Image.asset).

Tapping an exercise opens a modal bottom sheet (use showModalBottomSheet with isScrollControlled: true) containing:

The GIF (Image.asset, animating).
Exercise name and instructions.
A timer selector — default 30s, adjustable via a slider or preset chips (15s, 30s, 45s, 60s, 90s).
A large "Start Training" button.
When tapped: start a countdown displayed in the modal (large monospace text). On completion, fire a local notification ("Exercise complete — {name}") and show an in-app completion state (SnackBar + visual change in the modal).
If youtubeUrl != null, a "Watch on YouTube" button that opens it via url_launcher (LaunchMode.externalApplication).
Timer must use Dart's Timer.periodic or Stopwatch — no external timer packages.

Critical: If the user backgrounds the app or dismisses the modal while the timer runs, the countdown must still complete and fire the notification. Schedule the completion notification via flutter_local_notifications with zonedSchedule at now + duration as a fallback, and cancel it if the modal timer completes normally while app is foreground.

FEATURE 2: DIET TAB
Purpose: Browse meal combinations filtered by calorie range.

Data model (hardcoded in lib/data/seed/meals.dart):

dart
class Meal {
  final String id;
  final String name;
  final int calories;           // total kcal
  final String description;     // short text
  final List<String> ingredients;
  final String category;        // e.g. Breakfast, Lunch, Dinner, Snack
}
Provide at least 25 sample meals spanning ~300 to ~900 kcal.

UI:

Top: a calorie range filter — a RangeSlider (e.g. 0–1000 kcal) plus category chips (Breakfast / Lunch / Dinner / Snack / All).

List of meal cards showing name, calories (prominent badge), category, and short description.

Tapping a card opens a detail sheet/modal with full description, ingredients list, and calorie breakdown. No actions beyond viewing and dismissing.

FEATURE 3: SCHEDULE TAB (CRUD + NOTIFICATIONS)
Purpose: User creates weekly workout schedule entries. This is the only feature with persistent user data.

SQLite schema (schedule_items table):

text
id           INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT
day_of_week  INTEGER NOT NULL        -- 1=Mon ... 7=Sun
time_hour    INTEGER NOT NULL        -- 0-23
time_minute  INTEGER NOT NULL        -- 0-59
title        TEXT NOT NULL           -- e.g. "Leg Day"
description  TEXT                    -- optional notes for that day
created_at   INTEGER NOT NULL        -- epoch millis
Use sqflite. Provide a ScheduleRepository with insert, update, delete, getAll, getByDay.

UI:

Grouped list by day of week, styled like a clock/alarm app — each day is a section header, entries below it are cards showing time (HH:mm) and title.

FAB or top-right "+" button opens a Create Schedule form (full-screen dialog or bottom sheet):

Day picker (dropdown or segmented buttons for Mon–Sun).

Time picker (showTimePicker).

Title text field (required).

Description text field (multiline, optional).

Save button.

Tapping an existing schedule item opens the same form pre-filled in edit mode, with a Delete button (confirm with an AlertDialog before deleting).

Notification scheduling:

On create/update, schedule a repeating weekly notification via flutter_local_notifications zonedSchedule using DateTimeComponents.dayOfWeekAndTime, at the schedule time minus the user's reminder offset (see Account tab). Cancel and reschedule on edit.

On delete, cancel the corresponding notification by ID (derive a stable notification ID from the DB row id).

On app launch, resync all notifications from the DB (in case of reboots or permission changes).

FEATURE 4: ACCOUNT TAB
Purpose: App-level preferences only. No login, no sign out.

Sections:

Appearance — Light / Dark / System theme toggle. Persist via shared_preferences and apply immediately.

Notifications —

Master toggle to enable/disable all reminders (uses flutter_local_notifications + runtime POST_NOTIFICATIONS permission on Android 13+).

Reminder lead-time selector: 30 min / 1 hr / 6 hr / 12 hr before workout. Default 1 hr. Persist this value and resync all scheduled notifications when changed.

A note in the UI explaining that if permission is denied, reminders will not fire.

About — App name, version, small static description. No "sign out" button.

Handle the case where notification permission is denied: show a banner in the Schedule tab and a message in Account with a button to open system settings (openAppSettings from permission_handler, or a manual intent).

ANDROID CONFIGURATION CHECKLIST (agent must do these)
android/app/build.gradle (or .kts):

Enable core library desugaring:

gradle
compileOptions {
    coreLibraryDesugaringEnabled true
}
dependencies {
    coreLibraryDesugaring 'com.android.tools:desugar_jdk_libs:2.1.4'
}
minSdkVersion must be at least 21 (recommend 23).

android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml:

<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS"/>

<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM"/>

<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED"/>

Register flutter_local_notifications receivers for scheduled notifications and boot.

Add the <queries> block for url_launcher (YouTube intent) per url_launcher docs.

